WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 283: Walt Disney: Mickey Mouse, Snow White's Folly, and Disneyland

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:20.480
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series celebrates the Midwestern dreamer whose imagination, artistic daring, and technological innovation created the golden age of family entertainment.

4
00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:26.640
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary life of Walt Disney.

5
00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:40.080
[Rebecca] Born in Chicago in December 1901 to Elias and Flora Disney, young Walter Elias Disney discovered his idyllic creative sanctuary when his family relocated to a small farm in Marceline, Missouri.

6
00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:51.520
[George] Roaming the pastoral apple orchards and weeping willows, Walt fell in love with farm animals, spending hours sketching pigs, horses, and barnyard mice with charcoal and crayons.

7
00:00:51.520 --> 00:01:05.040
[George] When hard times forced the family to Kansas City, nine-year-old Walt and his older brother Roy woke at 3:30 every morning in freezing blizzards to deliver heavy bundles of newspapers for their stern, puritanical father.

8
00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:14.640
[George] In Kansas City, Walt discovered the emerging magic of movie theaters and comic strips, enrolling in weekend classes at the Kansas City Art Institute.

9
00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:30.960
[George] After driving an ambulance for the American Red Cross in France following the armistice of World War One, decorating his canvas truck with humorous cartoon caricatures, Walt returned to Kansas City to launch his first animation company, Lafogram Films.

10
00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:35.520
[George] Operating out of a cramped second-floor walk-up on East 31st Street.

11
00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:41.600
[George] Young Walt's childhood in Marceline was an idyllic Eden that permanently shaped his artistic imagination.

12
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:48.480
[George] He befriended a retired German physician, Doc Sherwood, who gave him drawing pads to sketch his horses.

13
00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:58.160
[George] And spent hours watching the roaring steam locomotives of the Santa Fe Railroad rumble through town, an obsession with trains that persisted throughout his adult life.

14
00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:11.960
[George] In Kansas City, his tyrannical father Elias confiscated all of Walt's delivery wages, leaving the young boy to secretly take on morning delivery routes for a pharmacist and candymaker to earn pocket change for art supplies.

15
00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:24.920
[George] His experiences driving Red Cross ambulances through shattered French villages following the armistice taught him the profound psychological need for humor, laughter, and whimsy in a traumatized post-war world.

16
00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:43.000
[George] When Laffogram collapsed into bankruptcy in 1923, 21-year-old Walt packed a cardboard suitcase, bought a one-way train ticket to Los Angeles with $40 in his pocket, and partnered with his financially prudent brother Roy to found the Disney Brothers cartoon studio.

17
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:55.000
[George] Alongside his genius animator and lifelong friend Ub Eworks, Walt created Oswald the Lucky Rabbit, a mischievous, rubbery animated star that scored an immediate hit with moviegoers.

18
00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:05.160
[George] In early 1928, Walt traveled to New York to renegotiate higher production budgets with distributor Charles Mintz, only to suffer a crushing betrayal.

19
00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:11.240
[George] Mintz revealed that he had secretly poached Disney's animators and held the legal copyright to Oswald.

20
00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:16.120
[George] Offering Walt an ultimatum to accept a drastic pay cut or be forced out.

21
00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:21.880
[George] Refusing to surrender his creative independence, Walt walked out of Mintz's office with nothing.

22
00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:28.280
[George] On the long three-day train ride back to California, Walt sat at a drawing pad and sketched a perky.

23
00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:41.000
[George] Round-eared mouse with white gloves and red shorts, christening him Mortimer, until his wife Lillian insisted that the name was too pompous, suggesting instead the friendlier, bouncy moniker, Mickey.

24
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:57.680
[George] The devastating loss of Oswald the lucky rabbit to Charles Mintz was the defining business crucible of Disney's young career, teaching him a ruthless lesson about copyright ownership and corporate piracy that made him obsessively guard his artistic properties for the rest of his life.

25
00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:13.760
[George] During the agonizing, three-day journey aboard the Santa Fe Chief train from New York to California, while his brother Roy waited anxiously and Lowe's Angels for news of their contract renewal, Walt channeled his acute emotional grief into frenzied creative sketches.

26
00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:28.880
[George] Lillian Disney's rejection of the name Mortimer Mouse as excessively stuffy and pretentious saved the character from obscurity, suggesting instead the cheerful, democratic, everyday name Mickey that echoed the common man of the American heartland.

27
00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:36.000
[George] Determined to surpass his competitors, Disney recognized that sound was the revolutionary frontier of cinema.

28
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:47.680
[George] While early talkies were crude novelties, Walt and Ub Iwerks conceived an animated short where the sound effects, character voices, and music were synchronized frame by frame with the visual action.

29
00:04:47.680 --> 00:05:05.040
[George] On November 18, 1928, Steamboat Willie premiered at the Colony Theater in New York City, featuring Mickey Mouse whistling cheerfully at the helm of a paddlewheel steamboat, turning cow's teeth into xylophones and pulling cats' tails to the lively rhythm of Turkey in the straw.

30
00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:12.240
[George] Providing Mickey's high pitched falsetto voice himself, Walt's cartoon was an explosive international sensation.

31
00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:18.640
[George] Audiences stood on their seats cheering, demanding the projectionist replay the short before the main feature.

32
00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:34.440
[George] Mickey Mouse became a global cultural phenomenon, followed by the pioneering Silly Symphony series, where Walt introduced full three-strip Technicolor to animation in 1932's flowers and Trees, winning the first of his record 22 Academy Awards.

33
00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:51.880
[George] To achieve frame-by-frame synchronization between animation and the soundtrack, Walt and Ub Iworks constructed an ingenious optical metronome system, drawing bouncing balls on the margins of the film's strip to establish a precise tempo of 24 frames per second for the orchestra.

34
00:05:51.880 --> 00:06:04.120
[George] When Walt traveled to New York to record the soundtrack with Pat Power's Cinephone system, the initial recording session was an expensive catastrophe because the musicians could not keep time with the visual action.

35
00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:20.120
[George] Walt pawned his personal roadster and his brother Roy sold his car to finance a second recording session with a live synchronized visual click track, producing a technological triumph that established Disney as the undisputed pioneer of animated motion picture sound.

36
00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:33.880
[George] In 1934, Walt gathered his staff on a darkened studio soundstage and performed the entire story of Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs, acting out each dwarf's mannerisms and voice for four spellbinding hours.

37
00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:47.000
[George] Hollywood moguls, including his own brother Roy, were convinced the project was suicidal, mocking it as Disney's folly and arguing that adults would never sit through an 80-minute cartoon about fairy tale dwarfs.

38
00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:56.760
[George] Walt mortgaged his home, pushed production budgets past an unheard-of 1.5 million dollars, and pioneered revolutionary artistic technologies.

39
00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:09.240
[George] He established art anatomy classes for animators, invented the massive multiplane camera to create breathtaking three-dimensional depth, and demanded genuine emotional pathos from drawn characters.

40
00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:16.400
[George] Premiering at the Carthay Circle Theater in December 1937, the film moved Hollywood royalty to tears.

41
00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:21.120
[George] Charlie Chaplin and Clark Gable openly wept during Snow White's funeral.

42
00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:28.400
[George] And the film became the highest-grossing sound motion picture of its era, inaugurating the golden age of animation.

43
00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:42.320
[George] The production of Snow White was a heroic leap into uncharted artistic territory that nearly bankrupted the young studio, requiring over 700 artists to produce two million individual watercolor sketches and celluloid frames.

44
00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:45.840
[George] Walt insisted on absolute anatomical realism.

45
00:07:45.840 --> 00:08:04.880
[George] He brought live woodland animals into the studio for animators to study muscle movements, hired champion dancers to model Snow White's delicate waltzes, and directed the voice casting with meticulous precision, rejecting hundreds of famous actresses before discovering 18-year-old Adriana Caeslotti.

46
00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:19.760
[George] When Hollywood executives at the Carthay Circle premiere wiped away genuine tears as the seven dwarfs knelt in mourning beside Snow White's glass coffin, animation was forever elevated from juvenile slapstick into enduring classical cinematic art.

47
00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:40.640
[George] Channeling Snow White's box office fortunes into technical perfection, Disney produced a dazzling sequence of cinematic masterpieces, Pinocchio's luminous water effects, Fantasia's revolutionary blend of classical music and avant-garde visuals with stereophonic pantasound, and the poetic, watercolor beauty of Bambi.

48
00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:47.520
[George] However, the catastrophic closure of European markets during World War II plunged the studio into deep debt.

49
00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:59.920
[George] In May 1941, Disney was blindsided by a bitter, five-week animators' strike over unionization and wage disparities, an emotional trauma that permanently hardened Walt's paternalistic worldview.

50
00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:19.400
[George] During the war, Disney converted 90% of his Burbank studio to producing military training films, instructional shorts, and antinazi propaganda such as Dare Furus Face, starring Donald Duck, proving that animation was a vital instrument of national defense and democratic morale.

51
00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:36.120
[George] In Pinocchio and Fantasia, Disney pushed the technical boundaries of animation to breathtaking summits, utilizing the multiplane camera to soar over European alpine villages and visualizing Leopold Stakowski's classical orchestrations with abstract color symphonies.

52
00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:52.280
[George] The bitter animators strike of 1941, led by former star animator Art Babbitt, was an agonizing emotional shock that devastated Walt's paternalistic conviction that his studio was one happy family, causing him to weep openly in executive meetings.

53
00:09:52.280 --> 00:10:11.720
[George] During the war years, Disney's studio became an armed federal military encampment, housing anti-aircraft troops guarding nearby aircraft plants while animators produced over 200,000 feet of military training films, instructional manuals, and morale-boosting propaganda for the war department.

54
00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:25.320
[George] Sitting on park benches on Sunday afternoons watching his young daughters Diane and Sharon ride dusty, dilapidated merry-go-rounds, Walt dreamed of a clean, magical family park where parents and children could have fun together.

55
00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:41.640
[George] Hollywood executives dismissed the idea as an amusement park disaster, but Walt forged ahead secretly, forming wed enterprises and borrowing against his personal life insurance policies to purchase 160 acres of orange groves in Anaheim, California.

56
00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:54.160
[George] Opening on a sweltering Sunday, July 17, 1955, before a live national television audience of 90 million viewers, Black Sunday was plagued by logistical chaos.

57
00:10:54.160 --> 00:11:01.200
[George] Counterfeit tickets flooded the gates, women's high heels sank into wet asphalt, and drinking fountains failed.

58
00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:16.080
[George] Yet within weeks, Disneyland captured the heart of the world, welcoming kings, presidents, and millions of families into Main Street, USA, Fantasyland, and Tomorrowland, forever inventing the modern themed entertainment industry.

59
00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:31.920
[George] Walt's revolutionary concept of Disneyland discarded the cheap carnivals, grimy midway games, and safety hazards of traditional amusement parks, replacing them with immaculate, cinematic architectural environments where families could escape into living storybooks.

60
00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:35.840
[George] Walt supervised every architectural detail personally.

61
00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:38.800
[George] He designed Main Street, USA.

62
00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:53.760
[George] At 58 scale to create a welcoming, nostalgic intimacy, insisted that costumed characters maintain strict theatrical illusions, and created the legendary imagineering division to invent advanced hydraulic audio animatronics.

63
00:11:53.760 --> 00:12:05.200
[George] Despite opening day plumbing leaks and sweltering summer heat, Disneyland welcomed its one millionth visitor in just seven weeks, transforming Anaheim into a global tourist capital.

64
00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:17.760
[George] In the 1960s, Disney conquered live-action cinema with Mary Poppins, winning five Academy Awards, and captivated millions on the hit weekly television anthology The Wonderful World of Color.

65
00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:21.360
[George] But his final, grandest vision was the Florida Project.

66
00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:37.160
[George] Acquiring 27,000 acres of wilderness near Orlando to construct Walt Disney World and EPCOT, the experimental prototype community of tomorrow, a futuristic, planned urban city dedicated to technological innovation and human progress.

67
00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:47.960
[George] In November 1966, Walt was diagnosed with advanced lung cancer, passing away on December 15, 1966, at age 65.

68
00:12:47.960 --> 00:12:51.320
[George] Eric Severid eulogized him with poetic truth.

69
00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:57.880
[George] He was an original, not an artist, perhaps, but a creator with a child's eyes and an emperor's will.

70
00:12:57.880 --> 00:13:10.200
[George] Walt Disney's enduring legacy is written in the joy of billions across the globe who look up at the castle spires, hear the opening bars of When You Wish Upon a Star, and remember that dreams can indeed come true.

71
00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:20.840
[George] Walt's clandestine acquisition of 27,000 acres of Central Florida swampland through Phantom Dummy Corporations was an extraordinary feat of corporate secrecy.

72
00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:28.120
[George] Designed to prevent real estate speculators from driving up land prices before Disney could reveal his master plan.

73
00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:42.600
[George] In his final filmed presentation, recorded weeks before his death, Walt laid out his grand vision for EPCOT as a living laboratory of futuristic technology, automated mass transit, and sustainable urban living.

74
00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:56.840
[George] Walt Disney's enduring gift to humanity was not merely commercial empire, but the triumphant validation of childlike wonder, proving that the human heart will always yearn for stories of hope, courage, and happily ever after.

75
00:13:57.560 --> 00:14:00.680
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

76
00:14:00.680 --> 00:14:09.720
[Rebecca] Walt Disney's boundless imagination, artistic courage, and relentless pursuit of excellence redefined global storytelling and entertainment.

77
00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:18.720
[Rebecca] Join us next week as Rebecca returns with the digital revolution of Steve Jobs, from the garage in Lowe's Altos to the Macintosh and the iPhone.

78
00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:21.440
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

